💡 **SUGGESTION: the release advance is silently consumed when a dev-lane armed PR sits ahead of the validated candidate in FIFO order**

The gh-952 release sets `deadHeadPassed = true`, letting the first `ai_validated` candidate advance past the externally-merged holder. But inside the FIFO loop, the armed-head branch unconditionally resets `deadHeadPassed = false`:

```javascript
// An armed PR is a LIVE head by default: it owns the FIFO position
// and nobody advances past it — unless the stale-pending check
// below declares it dead (gh-766).
deadHeadPassed = false;
```

That branch fires for a **dev-lane arm** too (`ai_validating` without `pr_approved`) — which everywhere else in this file is explicitly NOT a merge-window slot holder (mutexAmong parity; see the red-holder diagnostic and the PR description: "a dev-lane arm never held the window"). So in the shape `[dev-armed pr-100 (ai_validating, unapproved), validated candidate pr-101]` with a fresh merged holder, the release line is logged but pr-101 never advances this run — it waits for the next event/tick even though nothing holds the window.

This is a rare corner (the incident shape had nothing armed at all, which is covered), the release stays visible in the log, and the next self-tick/event re-drives the queue, so it self-heals. If you want strict parity, gate the reset on the arm actually owning the window:

```javascript
if (labels.indexOf('pr_approved') !== -1) deadHeadPassed = false;
```

Not blocking — flagging for awareness since gh-766 has the same shape and chose "an armed PR is a LIVE head by default".

💡 **SUGGESTION: comment-failure swallow breaks diagnostics parity with the sibling unpark paths**

`clearStalePark952` drops the park-clear comment failure silently:

```javascript
} catch (eClrSay952) { /* the label clear is the load-bearing part */ }
```

All three sibling unpark paths in this ladder (`headChangedNoVerdictVf`, `machineUnparkVf`, the human-push reset) log a `console.warn` when their `postComment` fails. This path exists precisely for the probe-outage shape — the scenario most likely to also fail a comment write — so a warn is arguably more valuable here than anywhere else:

```javascript
} catch (eClrSay952) {
